{
  "diagnosis": "The episode failed before acquisition. Depth probing usefully established target-region and tabletop geometry, and the policy checked the hand's clearance above the table. However, it treated a hand-clearance preview as sufficient justification for an orientation-only leveling move. The executed motion brought the proximal arm into the torso and triggered an immediate abort. Endpoint IK accuracy did not establish collision-free whole-arm motion or reliable attitude tracking. The object remained upright on the table, untouched and unlifted. The principal failure was unverified self-clearance during reorientation, not incorrect object localization.",
  "failure_mode": "Proximal-arm self-collision during pre-acquisition reorientation, caused by substituting local hand/table clearance and endpoint reachability for whole-arm motion safety.",
  "lessons_review": [
    {
      "lesson": "Before reducing clearance to a support or obstacle, identify which part of the entire moving assembly would reach that boundary first, including intermediate hand links, arm links, and any held object.",
      "verdict": "helped",
      "why": "At step 1, the policy explicitly checked the lowest hand geometry, and no hand-table contact occurred. However, its application stopped at hand clearance despite the lesson mentioning arm links. The rule needs to explicitly cover the robot's own body and rotations at a fixed palm position."
    },
    {
      "lesson": "Keep a separate, visually verified, task-compatible attitude reference.",
      "verdict": "hurt",
      "why": "At step 1, the policy chose to level the hand in place to establish a repeatable attitude before demonstrating that the existing attitude was unsuitable. This followed the lesson's preference for orientation-only correction in presumed free space, but free space around the hand did not imply clearance around the shoulder. The revision should make attitude normalization conditional and allow coordinated position-and-attitude adjustments."
    },
    {
      "lesson": "Before probing depth, state which uncertainty the result will resolve and how it will change the next action.",
      "verdict": "helped",
      "why": "At step 1, the depth batch supplied useful target-region and support geometry for the clearance calculation. There is no evidence that robot returns were mistaken for target surfaces. This was useful local sensing, but it did not resolve the proximal-arm clearance uncertainty responsible for the abort."
    },
    {
      "lesson": "For acquisition, place the intended contact region between opposing contact surfaces before closing, and close without advancing into the object.",
      "verdict": "neutral",
      "why": "The episode ended during step 1 without closure, object contact, lifting, or holding. The acquisition and completion checks were never exercised and should be retained rather than changed based on an unattempted grasp."
    }
  ],
  "lessons": [
    {
      "lesson": "Before any motion, including rotation at a fixed palm position, assess clearance of the entire moving assembly against supports, obstacles, and the robot's own body using available views and measured joints. Hand height, a hand-only preview, and endpoint reachability do not establish proximal-arm safety. If the limiting region is hidden or the required joint motion is uncertain, inspect an informative available view or choose a short coordinated waypoint through observable free space that preserves a well-separated arm posture. Recheck measured joint changes and tracking before continuing.",
      "evidence_steps": [1],
      "why": "Both previews reported lowest-hand geometry, while the abort was caused by right_shoulder_yaw_link contacting torso_link. There was no hand-table contact. The small endpoint IK residual did not certify the actual whole-arm trajectory."
    },
    {
      "lesson": "Keep a separately verified, task-compatible attitude reference, choosing the current measured attitude when it is already suitable rather than leveling by default. Initialize position commands from the measured palm, but do not let tracking error silently redefine the attitude reference. When an attitude change is necessary, use clearance-checked incremental waypoints and allow coordinated translation when that preserves a safer arm posture than rotating in place. Compare measured attitude and joints with the intended motion; if tracking is poor, choose a stable attainable attitude and replan the interaction rather than repeatedly forcing the reference.",
      "evidence_steps": [1],
      "why": "The selected action was an in-place leveling maneuver, although a translation retaining the original attitude had also been previewed. The executed attitude diverged substantially from the request before the self-collision abort. A reference should support the task without becoming an unconditional normalization objective."
    },
    {
      "lesson": "Before probing depth, identify the uncertainty to resolve and the action that would change with the result. Select identifiable features in the current depth-source image, distinguishing target exterior from openings, background, support, and robot surfaces. Use robot returns only for robot geometry or clearance. Reuse established geometry while current observations remain consistent; once target and support geometry are sufficient, direct further sensing toward the unresolved contact or clearance boundary and use that information to choose the next adjustment.",
      "evidence_steps": [1],
      "why": "The initial probes provided useful target and tabletop information and also included robot-region returns. Repeating localization would not have addressed the failure; the missing assessment concerned the moving arm relative to the torso."
    },
    {
      "lesson": "For acquisition, place the intended contact region between opposing contact surfaces before closing, and close without advancing into the object. Test retention with a cautious, clearance-checked motion and verify that identifiable object features remain seated and move with the hand. Check task-compatible object attitude and required displacement relative to the support separately from closure and palm motion. If alignment or retention is uncertain, obtain an informative view and correct it; if unintended contact moves an unsecured object, unload while preserving support and realign. Return an unstable held object to stable support and reacquire it. Begin any required hold only after all task conditions are established, and recheck them before declaring completion.",
      "evidence_steps": [1],
      "why": "These checks remain necessary but were not tested: the object never contacted the fingers, left the support, or entered a retained hold. The observed failure supplies no reason to weaken the existing acquisition and verification rule."
    }
  ]
}
